Sun-Bleach Loom

Creative Catalyst: It was reverse-engineered from a corrupted cassette found in a time capsule.

Sun-Bleach Loom is a buoyant, high-fidelity collision of melancholic K-Indie chord progressions and the driving, industrial-adjacent repetition of Detroit techno, all filtered through the warm, magnetic saturation of 1950s magnetic tape. It sounds like a transistor radio dropped into a tropical garden—lush, slightly distorted, and relentlessly rhythmic. The Moog Minimoog provides thick, gooey basslines that anchor the airy, jangly guitars, creating a sound that feels simultaneously nostalgic for a time that never existed and urgent enough for a dance floor.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 122–128 BPM, featuring a "swinging" 4/4 kick drum that feels like a heartbeat skipping due to tape wow-and-flutter.
*   Harmonic Palette: Major 7th and 9th chords common in Korean indie pop, played with a mid-century "bop" jazz sensibility.
*   Production Signatures: Heavy use of spring reverb on vocal tracks, tape-speed modulation (warble) as a rhythmic element, and side-chain compression that mimics the mechanical "thump" of a reel-to-reel machine.
*   Dynamics: Tracks start with sparse, intimate guitar plucking before blooming into dense, layered synth-and-percussion cascades.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement coalesced in the humid coastal basements of Incheon, where local producers obsessed over a degraded tape salvaged from a 1950s time capsule. This "corrupted" artifact, containing fragmented jazz lounge recordings layered over mechanical factory noise, became the holy grail for a subculture that rejects digital cleanliness. Dressed in moth-eaten mid-century linen suits dyed in vibrant, tropical hues, the scene meets for "weaving parties" where they splice magnetic tape by hand, looping melodies until the physical medium physically unravels. The music is a ritual of mending—trying to recreate the ghost of the tape while dancing to the mechanical heartbeat of modern techno.

Central Instruments: Moog Minimoog (for sub-bass and lead whistles), Fender Jaguar electric guitars (clean, spring-reverb heavy), Roland TR-909 (processed through tube preamps to mimic 50s-era warmth), and vintage marimbas.

For Fans Of: Floating Points, Mid-Air Thief, Moodymann, Les Baxter.

